                        "language": "english",
                        "review": "Disclosure: review key provided by the developer.\n\nI first saw Professor Why at Hall of Games in Wrocław in 2014. It was by far the best of the show and my kids spent a good deal of time there. To my surprise they managed to port the game on PC and it can now work with just any webcam.\n\nThis is not exactly a game, but a toy which is best played by kids together with their parents. This cooperativity begins early, as you and your kid will have to prepare the set of cards youself. This requires a printer, scissors, and a pair of small hands to manipulate them. We also applied some glue and cardboard. Great! Now it was ready to launch.\n\nImmediately after running the game we noticed that our desktop is so much smaller than the large table available at the exhibition :). Also it turned out that in winter afternoons it quickly gets too dark for our webcam to recognize the card patterns efffectively. Last but not least, the game is a bit \"too fair\" and incorrect combinations get recognized as easy as correct ones, which can get frustrating for little chemists. These are all but minor technical issues. The only real problem is the scoring system. The very idea of substracting points for mistakes in a game for kids relying and showing experimentation is horribly wrong. It would be much better if the mistakes were officialy allowed and resulted in funny comments, reactions, even explosions :) since this is why we do chemistry on PC, not in father's garage or mother's kitchen, don't we. \n\nSo it's not a type of game which you can leave your kids with and go away... which is, actually, good again, for those of us who miss time spent with their kids. The play itself gives kids a good laugh and a big boost of chemistry knowledge (and why you should keep your working area clean and tidy :)). If you have curious kids, would like to spend time with them but they prefer the computer, you should give this game a try; it is - as far as I know - unlike any other here on Steam.\n\nEspecially if you have a large table in front of your web camera :)",
